      'IPHEX 2026 runs from 6 to 8 September across multiple halls at Bharat Mandapam in Pragati Maidan, and the single most useful thing an exhibitor can do before designing anything is to accept what kind of floor this really is. This is a sourcing environment rather than a consumer environment. The people walking your carpet are procurement heads from distributors in West Africa, registration consultants from Latin America, tender agents working on behalf of health ministries across Central Asia, private label buyers from Southeast Asia, and domestic manufacturers hunting for spare contract capacity. None of them arrived to be entertained. They came to shorten a supplier shortlist, and every decision you make about layout, lighting, graphics and staffing either helps them do that or quietly gets in the way. Because the show spreads across multiple halls, footfall behaves very differently from a single hall event. Buyers arrive with a printed hall map and a target list, they move in short bursts between scheduled meetings, and most of them will never walk the same aisle twice. In practice that gives your stand roughly eight seconds of peripheral attention in which to answer three questions: what therapeutic or product category do you actually make, which regulatory approvals do you hold, and what volume can you commit to within a realistic timeline. If a visitor has to step inside and interrogate a salesperson to learn those three facts, you have already surrendered the majority of passing traffic to the neighbour who printed them at fascia height in letters large enough to read from the far side of the aisle. The rhythm of the three days is equally predictable and equally worth planning around. The sixth of September carries inaugurations, official delegations, escorted buyer groups and a great deal of movement without much depth. The seventh is the densest buying day, when the substantive conversations happen and when your senior technical and regulatory people must be physically present on the floor rather than sitting in a hotel lobby waiting to be summoned. The eighth thins out noticeably by mid afternoon, yet the visitors who remain are frequently the most decision ready of the entire show, because they have completed their comparison rounds and returned to two or three suppliers to negotiate terms. Staffing your stand identically across all three days wastes payroll on the first day and loses revenue on the third. It is also worth remembering that IPHEX does not run in isolation this September. Bharat Health and Bharat Nutraverse Expo occupy Halls 4FF and 6 on exactly the same dates, and Sanjeevani takes Hall 2FF on 6 and 7 September, which means a genuinely large pool of healthcare buyers, practitioners and institutional purchasers is circulating through the same complex, the same entry gates and the same crowded food courts. Exhibitors who plan only for their own hall miss that adjacency entirely. A pharmaceutical exporter with a nutraceutical line, a contract manufacturer with wellness capability, or a device maker with a hospital facing product all have legitimate reasons to be visible to the crowd next door, and the cost of buying that visibility through signage, delegate bag inserts or a small satellite presence is a fraction of the cost of a second stand.',
      'Once you understand the buyer, the stand design problem becomes considerably easier to solve, because a pharmaceutical export stand at Bharat Mandapam has to do three jobs at once and most stands only attempt one. The first job is qualification at distance, and that belongs on the perimeter. Build a capability wall that a buyer can read while walking, carrying only the information that filters people in or out: dosage forms, therapeutic segments, plant approvals, market registrations, and annual capacity. Resist the temptation to print a full product list. A wall of two hundred molecules in six point type communicates nothing except that you did not know what to prioritise, and it forces the buyer to do work that your team should have done for them. The second job is depth on demand, and this is where interactive content genuinely earns its place rather than functioning as decoration. A touch table running a well built dossier explorer lets a buyer filter your portfolio by market, by therapeutic area, by dosage form and by registration status, then generate a shortlist that is emailed to them before they leave the stand. A regulatory approval map, rendered as a large screen with animated territory highlighting, answers the question every export buyer asks within the first two minutes and answers it faster than any brochure. A short, well shot virtual walkthrough of your manufacturing facility, displayed on a large panel with headphones or directional audio, does something no printed photograph achieves: it lets a buyer in Nairobi or Bogota conduct a preliminary plant assessment without booking a flight, and it visibly demonstrates that you are comfortable being inspected. Keep these pieces short. Two minutes is the ceiling for anything a standing visitor will watch in a hall as loud and as busy as this one. The third job is negotiation, and it needs enclosure. Meeting pods with acoustic treatment, proper seating, a table large enough for laptops and samples, water, and a door that closes are not a luxury at a show where deal values run into crores and where competitors are physically twenty feet away. Budget at least a third of your floor area for these, and more if your commercial model depends on distributor agreements rather than spot orders. Around these three zones, several practical details separate professional stands from expensive ones. Sample display cases should be locked, lit and arranged by category rather than piled on a counter. Content should exist in at least English and one or two languages matched to your priority markets, because a Spanish or French landing page opened on a buyer phone builds more trust than any amount of English signage. Staffing needs choreography rather than headcount: one person positioned at the aisle edge to greet and qualify, technical specialists held in reserve inside, and a clear internal signal for handing a qualified visitor upward. Brief every team member on a single page covering the three questions above, the answers, and the three things they must never promise without approval. Finally, control your audio. In a packed hall, a stand playing loud video loops is not creating atmosphere, it is making its own meeting rooms unusable.',
