      'Activation design in the wellness category lives or dies on one principle: let people experience the product rather than hear about it, but engineer that experience so it produces information rather than only goodwill. Sampling is the obvious mechanic and the most frequently mishandled one. A table of paper cups staffed by a bored promoter generates a queue of people who want a free drink and nothing else. A properly designed sampling counter looks different in several specific ways. It is elevated and lit so the product, not the promoter, is the focal point. It carries a single hero variant rather than nine, because choice paralysis kills throughput on a busy aisle. It pairs every sample with one fact and one question, so the visitor learns something and your team learns something. It follows hygiene and food safety practice rigorously, with covered dispensing, gloved handling, temperature control for anything perishable, clear allergen labelling, and waste bins that are emptied before they overflow, all of which matter both for compliance and for the simple reason that a messy counter destroys the credibility of a health brand faster than any competitor can. Around the sampling, build content that gives visitors a reason to stay. Ingredient storytelling works well when it is physical: transparent columns showing the actual raw material, sourcing maps, and a short comparison of your extraction or blending method against the commodity alternative. Personalisation is the strongest digital mechanic available in this category, and it does not require heavy technology. A four or five question assessment on a tablet that asks about activity level, dietary pattern, sleep and specific goals, then recommends two products and emails the result with a discount code, converts far better than a spin the wheel game because the visitor receives something genuinely useful and you receive a profiled lead. Body composition analysers, grip strength stations and simple metabolic demonstrations pull crowds effectively in Halls 4FF and 6, though they need queue management, a defined result explanation script, and honesty about what the numbers mean. Augmented reality label scanning has a real use here as well, letting a visitor point a phone at a pack and see sourcing, testing and dosage information layered on top, which is far more persuasive than the same content on a poster. Keep the sensory environment deliberate. Wellness stands frequently make the mistake of borrowing the visual language of a nightclub, with dark structures, saturated colour and aggressive audio. Bright, clean, high colour rendering lighting, generous white or natural material surfaces and controlled sound make products look better and conversations easier. Throughput planning deserves attention as well, because both halls run at very different intensities across 6, 7 and 8 September, and a sampling counter designed for a quiet morning will collapse under a mid afternoon rush. Work out how many samples one promoter can serve in an hour, staff against the peak rather than the average, and pre portion stock in advance so that nobody is measuring powder while a queue builds. Finally, protect the trade zone. Behind the public facing activation, keep a seated area with pricing decks, margin structures, scheme documents and sample packs in retail ready form, and make sure a distributor never has to have that conversation standing in a queue of consumers holding sample cups.',
